WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.560
<v Instructor>So let's talk about health checks</v>

2
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:02.970
in Route 53.

3
00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:05.190
So health checks are a way for you to check

4
00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.770
the health of mainly public resources,

5
00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.060
although there's a way for us to do it

6
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.670
for private resources as well, as we'll see in this lecture.

7
00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:12.660
So the idea is that, for example,

8
00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.630
we have two load balancers in different regions

9
00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.940
and they're public load balancers, okay?

10
00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:18.773
And behind the scenes,

11
00:00:18.773 --> 00:00:20.700
we have our application running in both of them.

12
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.710
So we're running into a multi-region setup

13
00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.720
because we want high availability and so on,

14
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:25.980
at the region level.

15
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.700
Then, we're going to use Route 53 to create DNS records.

16
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.490
So that's when users access our URL, for example,

17
00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.910
MyDomain.com, then they get redirected to, for example,

18
00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:38.100
the closest load balancer they have.

19
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.550
So this would be the case with a latency-type of record.

20
00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.040
But we want to make sure that if one region is down,

21
00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.720
then we don't send our users

22
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.430
to that region obviously, right?

23
00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:49.530
So to do so, we're going to create health checks

24
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.000
from Route 53.

25
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.030
So we'll create health checks on the one in us-east-1

26
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:55.140
and we'll create a health check

27
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.560
on our instance in eu-west-1.

28
00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.940
Well, with these two health checks,

29
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.890
we're going to be able to associate them

30
00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.440
with our Route 53 records.

31
00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.160
And the reason we do so is to get automated DNS failover.

32
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.620
So we have three health checks that are possible.

33
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:11.490
The ones I just showed you

34
00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.260
which are the health checks that monitor an endpoint,

35
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.540
which is a public endpoint, so it could be an application,

36
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.090
a server, or another AWS resource.

37
00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:18.960
It could be a health check

38
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.430
that monitors other health checks,

39
00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.890
also called a calculated health checks.

40
00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:23.820
Or it could be a health check

41
00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.560
that monitors a CloudWatch alarm,

42
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.510
which gives you more control and is helpful

43
00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:30.090
for private resources, as we'll see in this lecture.

44
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.460
Finally, these health checks have their own metric

45
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.310
and you can view them in CloudWatch metrics as well.

46
00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.320
So let's look at how health checks work

47
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.280
with a specific endpoint.

48
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.880
So if we have a health check for eu-west-1 for an ALB,

49
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:44.220
then the health checkers of AWS

50
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.020
are coming from all around the world.

51
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.460
So it's not just one health checker,

52
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.040
it's about 15 health checkers from all around the world.

53
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.600
And they're all going to send requests

54
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.050
into our public endpoint to wherever route we set.

55
00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.630
And then, if it gets 200 okay code back,

56
00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:01.170
or the code we defined, then the resource is deemed healthy.

57
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:02.970
So about 15 global health checkers

58
00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:04.320
will check the endpoint health,

59
00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.380
and then you can set a threshold for healthy or unhealthy.

60
00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:09.630
You can set an interval, so we have two options.

61
00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.210
It could be either 30 seconds for regular health checks

62
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.430
or every 10 seconds, which is a higher cost,

63
00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.500
which is what's called a fast health check.

64
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.880
It supports many protocols, so HTTP, HTTPS, and TCP.

65
00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.420
And the rule is that if over 18% of the health checkers

66
00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.280
say that the endpoint is healthy,

67
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.530
then Route 53 will consider it healthy,

68
00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.690
otherwise it's deemed unhealthy.

69
00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:31.770
And you have the ability to choose

70
00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.410
which locations you want to use for the health checks.

71
00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:35.700
Now, the health checks will only pass

72
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.180
if you have the status 2xx or 3xx status code back

73
00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.980
from the load balancer

74
00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.830
and the health check has a cool capability.

75
00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.600
So if it is a text-based response,

76
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.700
then the health checkers can check

77
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.640
the first 5,120 bytes of the response

78
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.940
to look for some specific text in the response itself.

79
00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.430
Finally, very important from a network perspective,

80
00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.980
if you want for it to work, obviously,

81
00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.530
the health checkers must be able to access

82
00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.380
your application balancer or whatever endpoint you have.

83
00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.750
And so, therefore, you must allow incoming requests

84
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.750
coming from the Route 53 health checkers IP address range,

85
00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.490
and you can find this address range

86
00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.983
at the URL in the bottom right of the screen.

87
00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.560
Now, the second type of health checks we have

88
00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.480
are calculated health checks.

89
00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.130
And so, this is to combine the results

90
00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.470
of multiple health checks into a single health check.

91
00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:25.380
And so, if we look at Route 53, with three EC2 instance,

92
00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.180
we can create three health checks.

93
00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.590
They're all going to be children health check

94
00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.800
and they can all monitor each EC2 instance one by one.

95
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.960
And then, we can define a parent health check,

96
00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.460
which is going to be defined

97
00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.100
on all these child health checks.

98
00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.410
And so, the conditions to combine all these health checks

99
00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.110
could be an or, an and, or a not.

100
00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:47.160
You can monitor up to 255 child health checks

101
00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.260
and you can specify how many of the health checks

102
00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:51.810
need to pass to make the parent pass.

103
00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.880
So the use case for this, for example,

104
00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.380
if you want to have a parent health check

105
00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.120
to perform maintenance on your website

106
00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:00.210
without causing all the health checks to fail.

107
00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:03.720
And so, how do we monitor the health of a private resource?

108
00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.690
So in case you want to monitor something private,

109
00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.070
it's going to be difficult because,

110
00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:09.960
well, all the Route 53 health checkers

111
00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:12.810
live on the public web and they're outside of your VPC.

112
00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:14.730
So they cannot access private endpoints,

113
00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:18.210
so if it's a private VPC or an on-premises resource.

114
00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:19.890
And so, the way we can do it, though,

115
00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.960
is to create a CloudWatch metric

116
00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.090
and assign a CloudWatch alarm on it.

117
00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.980
And then, you can assign the CloudWatch alarm

118
00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:27.240
into the health checker.

119
00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.280
So the idea is that we're going to monitor the health

120
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.710
of our EC2 instance in a private subnet

121
00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:32.760
with a CloudWatch metric,

122
00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.860
and then, if the metric is breached,

123
00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.290
we're going to create a CloudWatch alarm on it.

124
00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.840
And when the alarm goes into the alarm state,

125
00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:40.920
then the health checker

126
00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.140
is going to be automatically unhealthy,

127
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.300
and therefore, we'll have created exactly what we want,

128
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.180
which is a health check on a private resource,

129
00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.490
which is the most common use case on how to do it.

130
00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.890
So that's it for this lecture, I hope you liked it.

131
00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.840
And I will see you in the next lecture.

